First, we will create one or more unified kernel image(s) (UKI).

Normally, the boot loader will execute what is called an EFI boot stub, this is
a kernel image packaged as a UEFI executable. Arguments can be passed to the
kernel via the commandline, which can be set either directly in the bootloader
or in its on-disk configuration. The EFI stub will load an initial RAM file
system (initramfs, also called initial RAM disk, initrd) into memory. The
initramfs contains the necessary drivers and programs to bootstrap the system.

A unified kernel image packages the kernel, initramfs and commandline
parameters into a single EFI executable. This makes the process more manageable
and also allows usage of features like Secure Boot as it reduces the number of
files involved in the early boot stage dramatically.

To create a UKI, we will adapt the configuration and preset files for
mkinitcpio and write our kernel commandline to a designated file. This script
should take care of most of the work but each config file will be opened with a
text editor ($EDITOR) so you can make custom adjustments.

One thing to note is that we will be using a systemd based initramfs. ArchLinux
by default uses busybox but using systemd gives us access to a couple of handy
extensions during the boot process.
